task: bench-spark-lane
agent: opus-flink
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/bench-spark-lane
worktree: ../okay-wt-spark-lane

why: the operator asked for Spark in §20's table (2026-09-10), after
  fs2/zio/kyo. It is the fifth engine and the second DISTRIBUTED one,
  and okay-spark already carries the bridge the row would use —
  `SparkInterop.aggregateByKey` hands an okay `Aggregator` to Spark as
  its (zero, seqOp, combOp) triple, the same value Flink takes through
  `toFlink` and the JDK through `Collect.collector`.

what: `SparkLane`, the same five stages on an RDD in local mode, and a
  row in the whole-field table. Spark's answer to this job is a BATCH
  one — no event time, a window is a key, exactly the JDK's
  `groupingBy` shape but distributed — and the bunching stage needs the
  arrival INDEX carried through the shuffle, because an RDD has no
  encounter order to preserve. The same eleven checksums, asserted.

  THE BUILD RISK, named up front: okay-spark needs a two-stdlib test
  classpath (Spark is 2.13-only and its `SparkSession` lookup goes
  through Scala 2 runtime reflection). The first thing to try is the
  RDD API alone — `new SparkContext`, spark-core only — which may not
  need that lookup at all. If it does, the lane moves to okay-spark's
  own tests and prints its own table rather than dragging a mixed
  stdlib onto okay-flink's.

predict: slow per event (a shuffle per stage in local mode) and
  correct; the interesting row is not the throughput but that the same
  aggregator value ran on a fifth engine unchanged.
